# Lighting capabilities and colour

Choose the smallest control that does the task: a quick on and off action, a brightness adjustment, a tunable-white setting or native colour selection. Room and scene controls need a stated scope, because their lights can differ.

The examples on this page are simulated. They do not connect to Home Assistant or operate equipment.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-004-capabilities">Task and capability contract</h2>

Show a control only when the entity supports it. Home Assistant reports what a light supports in `supported_color_modes`, with `min_color_temp_kelvin` and `max_color_temp_kelvin` for tunable white.

| Capability | What to show |
| --- | --- |
| On and off only | The state and a named on or off action. No dimmer, colour wheel or kelvin field. |
| Brightness | The native brightness control with a readable percentage. Keep the integration’s conversion and its behaviour when off. |
| Colour temperature | The supported kelvin range and the current setting when the entity supplies it. |
| Colour modes | The native colour control that matches `supported_color_modes`. Do not assume every lamp does RGB. |
| Effects or transition | Only the effects the entity lists and the transition it supports. A running effect can limit other changes. |
| Group or room | Each member’s capabilities, any unavailable member and the lights the action will affect. Never assume every member supports what one member does. |

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-004-states">States, pending work and failure</h2>

Do not show brightness or colour as a current reading while a light is off. If a screen shows a remembered setting, label it as a setting, not as light being given out. A missing colour value while a light is off is not a white reading. An unavailable member stays unavailable inside a mixed group, and a group reporting on does not mean “Every light is on”.

While a change is in progress, keep the last reported state and mark the new setting as requested. A later report may come from another person’s change. If a scene or recipe request is accepted but one light does not report the target, show a partial result using [Action requests and results](action-results.html). Do not repeat a colour or transition command automatically to make the display agree.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-004-content">Content and interpretation</h2>

Keep “Theme colour” and “Light colour” visibly separate. The theme colour changes colour tokens only. It never changes a device. Give a swatch an accessible name or its numeric setting, because a coloured circle alone cannot show a light’s state. Keep effect and scene names exactly as supplied. Before a recipe runs, say whether it changes brightness, colour, power or several of them.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-004-accessibility">Accessibility and interaction</h2>

Keep the native colour input’s keyboard behaviour and give a text or detailed route for precise values. A colour preview must not be the only sign of the chosen setting. Let kelvin values, long device names and group results wrap. Check focus on a light swatch against a dark background. Do not use the chosen brand colour to stand for the colour of a device.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-004-integration">Native integration boundary</h2>

Use the Light domain and native card features with the entity’s own `supported_color_modes`, colour-temperature limits and feature flags. Home Assistant converts between colour modes, so a theme must not repeat those conversions. The Civica house lighting card and its recipe script have their own boundary. This page adds no generic colour picker and no new service call to that card.

